<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores related facts in tables: rows of values under named columns. It answers questions about them and applies changes under specified rules. A shop can ask which customer placed an order, what that order contains and whether it has been paid. It can also make “record the order and take the stock” one complete change, even while other customers are buying.</p>
<p>The tables are only part of the bargain. You choose what a row means, which invalid facts the database must refuse and how competing changes make their decisions. The application must handle interrupted requests; somebody must keep recoverable copies. Understanding those boundaries is what makes a relational database useful beyond storing a spreadsheet on a server.</p>
<p>Follow one small shop selling tea and tea things. Mina Shah places order 103 for one blue mug and two packets of green tea. It costs £29.00. There is only one mug left, and Tariq Aziz is trying to buy it too. We will give those facts a home, calculate an answer, protect the purchase and follow it through failure. The shop and figures are illustrative; the query results and competing-update outcomes were checked on PostgreSQL 18.6.</p>

