<h2 id="give-each-fact-a-home">Give each fact a home</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> represents one kind of fact. Each <strong>row</strong> is an instance of that fact; named <strong>columns</strong> hold its values. A row in <code>customers</code> describes one customer. A row in <code>orders</code> describes one placed order. A row in <code>order_items</code> describes one numbered line within an order, which we will call an <strong>order line</strong>.</p>
<p>The relational model calls a table-shaped collection of facts a <strong>relation</strong>. Its useful promise here is that facts have explicit identities and can be combined through matching values. You need not pack every fact shown on a screen into one stored row.</p>
<p>A <strong>primary key</strong> is the column or columns chosen to identify each row uniquely, with no missing key value. Customer 1 is Mina, and order 103 is hers. An order line needs both <code>order_id</code> and <code>line_no</code>: line 1 of order 103 is different from line 1 of order 101. This is a <strong>composite key</strong>, made of several columns.</p>
<p>A <strong>foreign key</strong> requires a reference to match an existing key. Order 103’s <code>customer_id = 1</code> points to Mina’s customer row; its line for P7 points to the blue mug’s product row. One customer can have many orders, and one order can have many lines and payments. Figure 1 draws those relationships and shows the resulting order.</p>
<Shop />
<p>The product’s current price and the line’s agreed price are different facts. Keeping <code>unit_price</code> on the line preserves the £20.00 agreement even if tomorrow’s mug costs £22.00. Copying Mina’s current name onto every order would instead create several copies of one changing fact, each needing correction.</p>
<p>The <strong>schema</strong> is the definition of tables, their values and their rules. A <strong>constraint</strong> is a rule the database checks when accepting a change. For example, an order-line quantity must be a whole number, present and positive:</p>
<Sql code="quantity integer NOT NULL CHECK (quantity > 0)" label="A column definition for a required positive quantity" />
<p><code>integer</code> chooses whole numbers, <code>NOT NULL</code> requires a value and <code>CHECK</code> rejects a value failing its condition. A zero-quantity line fails even if an import bypasses the shop’s form. This does not establish that the customer ordered the quantity supplied; the application still has to send the intended facts. <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a> develops identity, relationships, values and the rules a schema can enforce.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-an-answer-and-check-its-shape">Ask for an answer and check its shape</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language used to ask for and change rows in most relational systems. It is largely <strong>declarative</strong>: describe the result and let the engine, the software implementing the database, choose how to produce it. First say what one result row should mean. Here we want one row for order 103, with the customer’s current name and the sum of its line values.</p>
<p>A <strong>join</strong> combines rows whose values satisfy a condition. <code>SELECT</code> names output values, <code>FROM</code> names an input table and <code>WHERE</code> filters rows by a condition. The query below matches each order line to its order and each order to its customer. <code>SUM</code> adds the line values; <code>GROUP BY</code> combines the matched rows into one result per order and customer name.</p>
<Sql code={summary} label="Order 103: one result row, with its customer and line total" />
<p>PostgreSQL returned <code>103 | Mina Shah | 29.00</code>: £20.00 + 2 × £4.50. The short names <code>o</code>, <code>c</code> and <code>i</code> label the input tables. Matching a line to its one customer did not multiply that line. Adding another independent detail table can.</p>
<p>Order 101 has two lines, £4.50 and £35.00, and two payments, £30.00 and £9.50. Both totals should be £39.50. Join lines directly to payments by order, however, and each line matches both payments:</p>
<figure class="relational-figure">
 <table class="fanout-table"><caption>Order 101 · join each line to each payment</caption><thead><tr><th>line_no</th><th>Line value (£)</th><th>payment_id</th><th>amount (£)</th></tr></thead><tbody>{multiplied.map(row => <tr>{row.map(v => <td>{v}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table>
 <figcaption>Figure 2. Illustrative joined rows, checked against PostgreSQL. Each line and each payment appears twice: summing either amount column gives £79.00. This is an intermediate query result, not four stored order lines. The example includes only order 101, whose lines and payments are both present.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The wrong query returns £79.00 ordered and £79.00 paid. Its balance is zero, accidentally correct, so checking the balance alone misses the error. Check the contributing totals and the identity represented by each row.</p>
<details><summary>The wrong join, and the repair</summary>
 <Sql code={wrong} label="Wrong: independent line and payment details multiply" />
 <Sql code={correct} label="Correct for order 101: total each detail set before joining" />
</details>
<p>The repair totals lines and payments separately, each producing one row per order, then joins those totals. It returns £39.50 and £39.50. Order 103 has no payment, so this particular inner join, which keeps only matching rows, would omit it. A report of money still due must deliberately retain unpaid orders. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> develops an unpaid-order report in its own resettable fixture, then missing values, ordering and checked changes. Correctness comes before speed: making this query faster would leave its question wrong.</p>

<h2 id="reserve-the-last-mug">Reserve the last mug</h2>
<p>Rewind to just before order 103. Stock is one mug and twelve teas, already accounting for earlier orders. Accepting this pending order reserves its items by subtracting their quantities from that stock; taking payment happens later. Our rule is that the shop must not accept more new mug commitments than the stock available. Cancellation and payment failure would need a later protocol to release or complete a reservation.</p>
<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups database work into a change that is kept or discarded as a unit. <code>BEGIN</code> starts it, <code>COMMIT</code> asks to keep it and <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards it. Keeping all its database changes or none is <strong>atomicity</strong>. Mina must not lose stock if writing her order fails, or gain an order if reserving either product fails.</p>
<p>Atomicity alone does not settle competing decisions. Suppose both buyers read stock 1, calculate 0 in their applications, then write that calculated 0 and their orders. Both transactions can commit. Stock is non-negative, but two customers have been promised one mug. The quantity checks and foreign keys all pass.</p>
<p><strong>Isolation</strong> specifies how overlapping transactions may see and affect one another. At PostgreSQL’s default setting, <strong>Read Committed</strong>, an ordinary query reads changes committed before that query began. A later update does not automatically invalidate an earlier application decision. Figure 3 compares the stale assignment with a change that checks availability as it subtracts.</p>
<Race />
<p>The repaired update says “subtract one only if at least one remains”. A competing updater of P7 waits for the first transaction; after its commit, PostgreSQL rechecks the condition against the changed row. Tariq finds zero available and changes zero rows. His application must treat that result as refusal and roll back, rather than insert order 104 anyway.</p>
<p>Here is Mina’s complete database path for this new order. The comments describe required application branches; they are not SQL instructions that perform those branches. Every stock-taking writer must use the guarded subtraction, and both returned-row checks must pass before proceeding. This example fixes identifiers, prices and quantities to make the protocol visible.</p>
<Sql code={reserve} label="Order 103: conditionally reserve both products and record the order in one transaction" />
<p>If the mug or tea is unavailable, rollback discards the whole attempt, including any earlier subtraction. Any statement error must also lead to rollback. On success, stock becomes P7 0 and P3 10, and order 103 has both lines. A lost commit reply needs a further application rule, below.</p>
<p>This argument covers a condition on one product row at a time. Rules involving counts across several rows need a broader protocol. Different engines and isolation settings also permit different histories. <a href="/review/relational-trial-3/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops those choices and how to test the forbidden outcomes. Merely wrapping an arbitrary sequence in <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code> does not prove it safe.</p>

<h2 id="follow-the-work-behind-the-statement">Follow the work behind the statement</h2>
<p>The same SQL result can require very different work. A <strong>scan</strong> examines table rows to find matches. An <strong>index</strong> is an additional structure that helps locate rows by selected values. P7’s primary-key index gives PostgreSQL a route to its product row; on a tiny three-row table the engine may reasonably choose a scan instead.</p>
<p>Stored rows occupy <strong>pages</strong>, blocks read into memory together. PostgreSQL normally uses 8 KiB pages. Its cache holds copies of those pages so repeated work can avoid loading them again. An update changes a row in memory and records enough information to recover the change.</p>
<figure class="relational-figure work-path">
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Find P7.</strong> Choose a scan or an index route to the product row.</li>
  <li><strong>Change in memory.</strong> Check stock and subtract; another updater may wait here.</li>
  <li><strong>Make the commit recoverable.</strong> Record changes in a write-ahead log (WAL), a history from which the engine can reconstruct them. With ordinary durable settings, flush the commit’s log records to storage before confirming success.</li>
  <li><strong>Write data pages.</strong> Changed pages can reach their table files later. Crash recovery can replay the stored log if needed.</li>
 </ol>
 <figcaption>Figure 4. Illustrative PostgreSQL write path, based on its documented storage and log contract. It omits index maintenance, background work and detailed row versions; it is not a measured timeline. The underlying storage must honour its durability promises.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that an acknowledged commit survives the failures covered by that configuration. It does not require every changed data page to be written before the acknowledgement. Relaxing the commit’s wait for storage changes what a crash may lose; losing the whole storage device requires another copy.</p>
<p>A single-order lookup should ideally touch little data. A report adding every order’s value may need most of the order lines even when it returns one total. An index adds storage and work to changes, so creating one for every column is costly. PostgreSQL’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows its chosen execution plan, the operations used to answer a statement; <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> also runs it and reports observations.</p>
<p>Start a slow-request investigation by separating time spent waiting to reach the database, executing work, waiting behind another transaction and transferring results. Then inspect the responsible statements. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> follows that evidence through storage, indexes, plans and concurrent load.</p>

<h2 id="complete-the-application-request">Complete the application request</h2>
<p>Order 103 commits, but the reply to Mina’s browser is lost. The database has an order; Mina sees a timeout. If the database’s own commit reply is lost, even the application may be uncertain whether the order committed. A timeout reports the end of a wait, not the absence of a change.</p>
<p>Give this logical order attempt a stable identity before sending it. On repetition, reuse that identity and recover or coordinate the same result. Here order 103 can identify the attempt, provided it is assigned before the first attempt and tied to the same customer and items. Its primary key rejects a second order 103; that rejection alone does not return the original result or verify that the repeated input matches.</p>
<p>The application needs a complete outcome-resolution protocol, especially while the first attempt could still commit. Generating a fresh order identifier because the reply was lost would instead start another order. Keep Tariq’s losing 104 distinct from Mina’s repeated 103.</p>
<p>External fulfilment has another boundary. A database rollback cannot recall an ordinary request already accepted by a parcel service. Record the work to do with the order in the same transaction, then let a worker perform it with its own recovery rules. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> develops safe request replay, external work and bounded use of connections, the channels carrying application–database exchanges.</p>
<p>The contract also changes as the application evolves. Suppose the shop adds a delivery country. A new application cannot assume every old order has one as soon as the column exists. Old writers may still omit it. First add a representation old code can use, update writers, fill existing rows using justified values, verify completeness, then require the value. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns that compatibility sequence and its interruption plan. A schema change is unfinished while live applications disagree about its meaning.</p>

<h2 id="recover-the-right-state">Recover the right state</h2>
<p>At 10:10 on 20 October, a mistaken clean-up deletes paid order 101 with its lines and payments. Order 103 is validly placed at 10:15. The database has faithfully committed the mistake. Three recovery mechanisms answer different questions:</p>
<dl class="recovery-choices">
 <div><dt>Crash recovery</dt><dd>Bring the same database back after interrupted execution, preserving its committed work. It keeps the committed deletion.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Replica failover</dt><dd>Make another maintained copy, a replica, serve in place of a failed writer. A replica that has copied the deletion is missing 101 too.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Restore an earlier state</dt><dd>Reconstruct data from retained recovery inputs. PostgreSQL point-in-time recovery uses a base backup, a recoverable starting copy, plus saved log history, stopping before the mistake.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>Restoring to 10:09 recovers order 101 but predates valid order 103. Restore separately first, compare the histories and decide how to repair affected facts while accounting for later orders and external work. Replacing today’s whole database with yesterday’s correct copy can discard today’s correct purchases.</p>
<p>Give the service a named operating owner and explicit targets. An illustrative shop policy might allow five minutes of data loss after losing its location and require usable service within an hour. These are requirements, not measured results. A restore rehearsal must time retrieval, reconstruction, validation and application reconnection, then check known orders and their relationships. A successful backup job has not performed that rehearsal.</p>
<p>Access control limits who can read or change data; the operating owner also needs to detect failures and respond. A managed service may run backups and restore database files under its contract. The shop’s application owner must still check that recovered orders, payments and stock agree, account for later valid work, and decide when the application can resume taking orders. <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> develops that procedure: assign each task, rehearse the failure and check the data and application before returning traffic.</p>

<h2 id="put-data-and-decisions-in-the-right-place">Put data and decisions in the right place</h2>
<p>Suppose the application writes order 103 to one server, then immediately reads it from a replica that has not applied the change yet. The answer is “no such order” even though the write succeeded. <strong>Replication</strong> maintains copies of data; its timing determines how fresh each copy’s answers can be. Keep this immediate read on the writer, or use a supported way to wait for the replica to include the write.</p>
<p>Copying data does not divide its write ownership. Several read replicas can help suitable reporting reads, but ordinary changes to the same P7 stock still meet at the writer. A concrete reason to divide ownership would be measured write demand for independent product ranges exceeding one suitably tuned server’s capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Sharding</strong> assigns different subsets of data to different write owners. Splitting products can spread those independent changes, but Mina’s mug-and-tea order might now cross owners. Either a distributed transaction coordinates an all-or-nothing change across them, or the application must adopt different rules for accepting and repairing partly completed work. Location, routing and what happens when an owner cannot be reached become application-visible decisions.</p>
<p>A distributed relational system supplies some of that coordination within the database product. It still has a documented failure and freshness contract, communication delays and placement choices to test. Start with a demonstrated need for divided ownership or failure survival. The existing <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#choose-where-reads-and-writes-can-safely-run">operating discussion of replicas and partitions</a> supplies the next questions; <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> compares deployment boundaries.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-you-can-test">Choose a system you can test</h2>
<p>An engine’s guarantees depend on how it is deployed: a durable commit on one machine does not by itself survive losing that machine and its storage. Choose the engine alongside the arrangement for running it. Consider an illustrative shop with three application hosts sharing the same stock, a team able to operate one managed database service, and no demonstrated need for writes in several regions. Compare three shapes:</p>
<dl class="shortlist">
 <div><dt>Embedded · SQLite</dt><dd>The engine runs inside an application process and owns local files. It suits a single-device shop or local application. Directly sharing one SQLite WAL database between these three hosts fails its same-host access requirement. Placing SQLite behind one application service changes the candidate and leaves that service’s recovery to prove.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Conventional server · PostgreSQL</dt><dd>Applications share a database service through connections. Shortlist a managed PostgreSQL deployment for this shop, subject to the tests below. A single local server, including our verification server, does not establish survival of host loss.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Distributed relational service</dt><dd>Keep conditional on a demonstrated need to spread ownership or survive a specified wider failure while working. Compare placement, transaction delays and operational cost against a server arrangement meeting the same requirements.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>The current conclusion is a starting shortlist, not production acceptance. The first candidate is PostgreSQL because shared relational changes fit the application and a managed service fits the stated operating capacity. Disqualify it if the selected deployment fails a hard requirement. Keep each piece of evidence labelled: a manual documents a promise, a run measures one arrangement, and a forecast remains an assumption.</p>
<table class="evidence-matrix"><caption>What would make this shortlist defensible?</caption><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Evidence to obtain</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Facts and query meaning</td><td>Run the schema and critical queries; verify exact prices, missing values and relationship checks in the chosen engine.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Competing changes and durable acknowledgement</td><td>Race for the last mug and interrupt requests. Verify accepted identities after the failure the deployment claims to survive. Use <a href="/review/relational-trial-3/transactions/">transactions</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application recovery</a>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Workload, volume and geography</td><td>Measure order-taking together with reports at forecast and burst demand, from the intended locations. Include waiting and refused work. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Change and recovery ownership</td><td>Rehearse compatible application change and an isolated restore. Record times, loss and responsible people. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">live changes</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">operations</a>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Specialist features and ecosystem</td><td>Check required add-on features, application-library value conversions, permissions and support in the actual managed offering.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Cost, portability and exit</td><td>Price the passing arrangement including copies, backups, traffic and operating effort; export and re-import representative data, checking values and rules. Record engine-specific dependencies.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Change the workload and the shortlist can change. A report over years of order lines may favour a column store, which stores column values together for broad scans and calculations. The shop might keep transactional truth in PostgreSQL and feed a reporting copy, with an explicit freshness and recovery policy. A document store can suit independently changed documents; object storage suits product photographs; a search engine suits relevance-ranked discovery. Compare their actual contracts against the task, and account for maintaining another copy.</p>
<p>Begin with a disposable prototype and a small set of rejection tests. You can practise SQL while the production choice remains open. <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> carries the evaluation through a concrete candidate record, costs and an adoption decision. The branches below supply working depth when a question calls for it.</p>

